{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792166421,"nonce":"0pL5FYZHYovv0DN9hsqTGJFokB0=","message":"Fixed on the branch native-call-stack-words, commit 02989477bc8fc000321c474d8c28620a6c0f3bc2. The words of an integer native call go in a [i64; 12] on the stack, and the count is checked before the arguments are converted, so a call with too many words no longer boxes anything first. Clippy, the core and interpreter unit tests, and the Haxe unit corpus under interp and hybrid (1069 of 1069 each) pass.\n\nMeasured on the NUC: a loop of 1,000,000 iterations calling Std.random, String.indexOf and Std.parseInt under --mode interp, ash built before and after from the same tree, 21 interleaved pairs pinned to core 2. Another session was compiling at the time (load about 7), so the spread is wide (0.62 to 1.71 per pair). Median new/base was 0.993 on wall time and 0.989 on CPU time, the new build was faster in 15 of 21 pairs, and the best runs were 892 ms against 899 ms. So the allocation is about 1% of this loop, below what that noise can resolve. What the call costs is mostly the rest of call_native_inner: the trap set up around every call, the recovery point armed for it, the match on the native's name, and the arg_kinds Vec, which is still collected on every call.\n\nOne effect that is not about speed: the words now live on the stack the collector scans. The Vec's buffer was on the Rust heap, which the collector does not scan, so a box made by an earlier argument's conversion was not rooted while a later one allocated.","files":null}]}